# tlo: oblawa_mlodnik
On Saturday they rose in the dark. The frost was such that breath froze on men’s moustaches, and the snow creaked underfoot as loud as dry boards. The peasants of Zapadlina and Bukowiec stood in a long line at the edge of the field – several dozen men with rattles, with pots they beat with sticks, with pitchforks and axes. Before them the young wood showed black: spruces that over the last few years had crept out from the forest almost to the very fences.
At the gamekeeper’s signal they moved off. The rattles clattered, men shouted and whistled, and the echo rebounded from the wall of the woods as though another hunt were advancing behind the trees. Somewhere ahead the manor hounds were baying. Then the first shot boomed out. And a second. # pokaz: naganka
Jaromir walked at the very end of the line, where the young wood ran down to the frozen marshes.{ obietnica_antkowi: Antek kept close beside him, white as the snow.} Jaromir forced his way through a thicket and stopped dead.
In a small hollow among the spruces, beneath a fallen trunk, stood the white wolf. # pokaz: wilk
He was enormous – bigger than any wolf Jaromir had ever seen – and so thin that every rib showed beneath his coat. The ruff on his neck stood on end. Around one foreleg ran a ring of worn, sparser fur – in exactly the place where, years ago, Franek had borne three claw stripes that he swore came from a nail.
And the eyes… The eyes were human. Pale, with a clear pupil, and sad. They watched Jaromir from under the lowered head, and there was neither fear nor anger in them. Only weariness.
Behind him he heard the crunch of footsteps. Pawlik the gamekeeper was pushing through the spruces, his double-barrelled gun ready to fire. # pokaz: gajowy
# pytanie: What will Jaromir do?
